WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.520
- Free legal advice on the
internet is, shall we say, bad?

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.210
And unfortunately, some of the worst takes

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.434
come from one of my favorite
places on the internet,

00:00:08.434 --> 00:00:10.170
Reddit's rslash legal advice.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.440
And of course, on the other hand,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.770
anyone with an internet connection

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.870
knows the best headlines
always begin with Florida Man.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.510
So what happens when you combine

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.340
the subreddit legal advice and Florida?

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.744
Well, it gets interesting.

00:00:22.744 --> 00:00:25.700
(bright music)

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.350
And today's episode of bad legal advice

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.620
comes from Reddit user
Clean Transportation

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.830
who shares her own Florida man story.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.000
She writes, "Regular customer tipped me

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.070
scratch tickets for years.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.210
I finally won something

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.790
and they're threatening legal action

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.190
because I don't want to give them a cut."

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.757
Florida (chuckles) of course.

00:00:41.757 --> 00:00:43.770
"I worked as head bartender
for a local restaurant

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:44.630
for a number of years,

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:46.110
and there was a regular customer

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.790
who was there very frequently.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.520
I interacted with this
customer frequently,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.810
they tipped exclusively in scratch tickets

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.480
and specifically called
to ask for my schedule,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.110
asked me out on multiple
occasions, et cetera.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.940
Probably about 40 years my senior.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.380
If you've ever watched "The Good Place,"

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.970
this guy is IRL Brent.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:05.030
- It's all about taking
personal responsibility.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.120
I don't know if people do that.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.550
- Now, okay, so this
isn't exactly legal advice

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.460
but just general life advice,

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.190
please don't ask out your
bartenders who were just working,

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.020
don't mistake their professional courtesy

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.390
for genuine interest.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.960
And if a person has already said

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.860
they aren't interested in the first time,

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.460
they typically aren't charmed
if you ask them repeatedly.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.170
I hate to be the one to tell you this,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.210
but '80s movies kinda lied to you,

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.900
persistence in the face of no

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.560
isn't romantic in this
day and age, it's creepy.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.380
So come on, let's just not do that.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.987
But I digress, back to the post.

00:01:35.987 --> 00:01:37.720
"I won something from the scratch ticket

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:38.930
that Wasn't five, $10.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.200
The final amount after taxes

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.860
was about three months salary for me

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.490
and I was making good money.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.050
I specifically didn't tell the customer

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.610
not because I thought they would want it,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.320
but because I didn't want them feeling

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.450
like I owed them something,

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.140
or that I should be thankful towards them.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.100
I did tell a coworker

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.960
and it got to him through the grapevine.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.560
Next time I came in, he congratulated me

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.430
and asked me how we wanted to split it.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:00.480
I sternly told him that it was
my tip and long story short,

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.910
he went to my manager who took his side.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.670
I quit, had been planning to anyway,

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.320
since I'm about to finish school,

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.620
my manager gave this guy my email.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.280
I'm curious about the
legality of this too,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.080
and he, the customer emailed me saying

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.080
that if I didn't agree to give him 50%,

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:15.780
he'd take legal action."

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.950
So, here we have a female employee

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.220
dealing with a bar patron

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.650
who has repeatedly declared
a romantic interest in her,

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.500
which is not reciprocal,

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.190
threatens a lawsuit over a tip ticket,

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:26.480
and after all of that,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.470
the manager gives the bar
patron her email address.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.310
This is what we call in
the legal profession,

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.020
a total (beep) move,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.920
it's also the same in bird culture.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.130
But this probably isn't illegal.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.840
I know this isn't the
main part of the story,

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.160
but she's asking for legal advice here

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.000
and I just have to sink
my teeth into this one.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.380
So aside from information
classified as confidential

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:49.250
like an employee's medical history,

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.580
the disclosure of most
employee personal information

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.750
to third parties is largely unregulated.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.480
Many employers regularly share

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:56.780
their employee's personal information

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.530
with third party vendors
to run background checks,

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:02.050
to outsource administrative
functions, and so on.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.390
In Florida, which does
have a right to privacy

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.200
written into its state constitution,

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.970
does not have any specific laws

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.100
that would prevent an employer

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.600
from providing an employee's
personal information.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.380
And Florida laws against
invasion of privacy,

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.090
like intrusion or
publication of private facts,

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.330
typically requires showing

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.700
that the disclosure of such
information is offensive,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.370
or goes beyond all
possible bounds of decency.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.030
So it's not clear that
the manager giving out

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.700
an employee's email to a patron,

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.620
even one threatening to sue her,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.200
meets the offensiveness standard.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.620
And finally, another standard
caveat when I do these things,

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.860
this could totally be fake, I don't know.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.100
This could be completely
made up, I don't really care.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.220
This is fun to talk about,

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.730
it's an interesting legal issue.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:44.167
But anyway, back to the
putative Reddit post.

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:46.340
"Do I need to worry about
taking his threat seriously?

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:49.800
Is there anything that I can
or should do to protect myself?

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.160
I'm assuming this is a bluff

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:52.950
but I wanna have my bases covered.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.260
TL;DR me drink lady, mean
man who by many drinks

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.130
give me a scratchy
scratchy," okay, we get it.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.510
So she is worried that this
guy is going to sue her

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.310
for some or all of the lottery winnings.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:08.380
And this can be a complicated issue.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.850
Believe me, anytime the
lottery is involved,

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.890
lawyers are not far behind,

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.720
there is a long history
of lottery law out there.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.270
But before we dive into this
complicated, legal mess,

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:19.330
let's check in with the comments

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:21.030
to see if any of these Redditors

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.010
offered some good legal advice.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.620
And in fact, xShadey writes,
"Mmm, oong like green paper.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:29.700
Oong buy dizzy drink with
green paper, oong happy."

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.290
Yeah, so that's pretty much

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.040
about the quality of legal advice

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:34.060
that you're likely to get

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.410
when you ask for free
advice on the internet.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.600
It's basically random postings

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:40.554
from the "Unfrozen Caveman Lawyer."

00:04:40.554 --> 00:04:43.860
- Your world frightens and confuses me.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.310
- And that might be reason enough alone

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.190
for this redditor to retain counsel

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.130
because in America, of course,

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:52.620
you can sue anyone at any
time over just about anything.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.870
And even if a lawsuit
is completely meritless,

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.900
you still have to defend the case.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:01.558
But here since the "Unfrozen
Caveman Lawyer" is unavailable,

00:05:01.558 --> 00:05:05.920
RIP Phil Hartman, this Redditor
could hire a lottery lawyer.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.020
Yes, that's right,

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:10.250
there actually is a
thing called lottery law.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.372
A lottery lawyer can
help navigate tax laws,

00:05:12.372 --> 00:05:15.990
keep your identity anonymous
when you claim your prize,

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.120
and defend you from potential lawsuits

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.690
over the winnings like
this potential lawsuit.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.100
Because winning the lottery
can turn into a messy battle

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.370
that ruins friendships,
tears apart families,

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.800
and explodes into costly lawsuits.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.560
And this is especially the case

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.450
where you have the dreaded
workplace lottery pools.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:34.930
For example, in 2012,

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:38.290
12 co-workers in Illinois won $118 million

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:41.350
and they were sued by 11
of their other colleagues

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:44.540
who claimed that they were in
the pool and demanded a share.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.610
It took three years and six
law firms to settle the case

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.460
with the original 12,
getting $6 million each,

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.420
and six others splitting $13.8 million

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.470
but no one got a penny of that
until the case was resolved.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.030
And similarly, in 2009,

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.170
five construction
workers in a lottery pool

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:01.730
sued when the sixth co-worker

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:04.390
took a $38.5 million jackpot,

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:06.480
quit his job, and then disappeared.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:07.600
A judge ordered the defendant

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.210
to surrender $20 million to
the five former co-workers.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:12.730
That's why lottery lawyers

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.470
recommend all workplace lottery pools

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:16.290
have a sharing agreement in writing,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.850
because just like Biggie says
more financial remuneration,

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:22.670
more judicial proceedings
and legal ramifications.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:24.610
But let's dive back into the comments

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:26.770
maybe there's something
else that's better in here.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:28.587
For example, SignUpAccountz writes,

00:06:28.587 --> 00:06:30.270
"If you get sued, then
you'll need to show up

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.797
and comply with any judge's
orders," fair enough.

00:06:32.797 --> 00:06:34.430
"But right now you owe him nothing

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.980
and don't even have a responsibility
to reply to his emails.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:39.210
Do save them just in
case you get sued though,

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:40.760
but if he is threatening litigation

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.540
I would recommend ceasing all contact

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.740
from this point forward
without an attorney present."

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.960
And this is technically correct.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:51.320
- You are technically correct,
the best kind of correct.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.970
- Typically, you have no
legal obligation to respond

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.420
to pre-litigation demand
letters threatening a lawsuit.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:58.960
And if the bar patron hasn't
even retained a lawyer,

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.980
this could just be an empty threat.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:03.997
And then another Redditor,
Meowzebub chimes in helpfully,

00:07:03.997 --> 00:07:06.010
"And just to clarify
why this is important.

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:08.430
If this guy files a lawsuit
against the Redditor,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:09.930
which he is completely entitled to do

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.080
regardless of the lack of
legal basis for the claim,

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.353
she needs to show up or be
ruled against by default."

00:07:15.353 --> 00:07:17.530
And these comments are interesting

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:19.050
because they're not really
getting to the heart

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:19.930
of the substantive issue,

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.120
but they are really
interested in civil procedure

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.930
and you know, they're not wrong.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:24.860
And it's important to note

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:28.320
that a plaintiff isn't entitled
to file a meritless lawsuit.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.830
If a plaintiff files a lawsuit

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:32.230
that they know has no
legal basis whatsoever,

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:34.250
they can be subject to sanctions.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.490
But as we see time and time again,

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:39.130
people will often file
these meritless suits

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:40.170
and avoid sanctions

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.690
because it's rare for judges
to take that extra step,

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:47.360
and it's hard to weed out
the lawsuits that have merit

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.900
from the ones that are
completely meritless

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.290
at the first stages.

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:52.970
But Meowzebub, who has
a hilarious username,

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:54.640
does make a very important point.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.450
Even if the case is complete nonsense,

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:59.000
you still have to defend
yourself from the lawsuit

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.552
otherwise you could lose
automatically by default.

00:08:01.552 --> 00:08:04.076
- I guess you're the winner by default.

00:08:04.076 --> 00:08:05.750
- Default, woo-hoo!

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.040
- When a person is sued and served

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.200
with an official court
summons in a complaint,

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:12.060
the defendant only has
a limited amount of time

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.410
to file a response, either
what's called a motion to dismiss

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.470
or a formal answer to the complaint.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:19.040
If a defendant fails to file any response

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.010
or otherwise abandons the case,

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:22.480
the plaintiff can request the court

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.090
enter what's called the default judgment

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.970
against the absencee defendant.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:27.460
So that's why it's so incredibly important

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.520
to be actually represented by counsel

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:31.770
if you are formally sued.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.900
But back to the crux of this Reddit post,

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:34.810
the central question

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:36.360
is whether there is enforceable contract

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.420
between the bar patron and the bartender

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:40.050
to split the ticket winnings.

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:42.540
And this is really important
because most people assume

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:43.840
that a contract has to be written,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.440
but that's actually not the case,

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.730
only a small number of
contracts must be in writing.

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:52.370
And in fact, sometimes there
can even be an implied contract

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.900
based on the actions of the individuals.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.600
So it's not nearly as cut
and dry as it would appear.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:58.720
In fact, generally speaking,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.210
a contract doesn't have to be
in writing to be enforceable,

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:05.010
as both written contracts and
oral contracts can be valid.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.710
In fact, most lottery
lawsuits are based exclusively

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.840
on oral agreements to share winnings,

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:14.220
because people don't often
write down their contracts,

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.750
and you know, that keeps lawyers employed.

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:19.540
But all enforceable
contracts must have an offer,

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:22.210
an acceptance of that
offer, and consideration.

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:24.350
An offer is usually a specific proposal

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:26.350
to enter into an agreement with another.

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:28.790
Acceptance of that offer
is an assent to the bargain

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:31.110
which can either be expressed,
like signing an agreement,

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:32.470
or implied by conduct.

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:36.180
And consideration is
performance or a return promise

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.800
for which the party to the
contract has bargained.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.930
In order to constitute a
bargain for consideration,

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:44.180
both sides must negotiate
some benefit and/or detriment

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.200
from one another in exchange
for the promises to perform.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.150
However, a gift promise, like a tip,

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:51.740
lacks these required elements.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.890
When you promise someone a
gift, there's no formal offer,

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.960
there's no offer to accept,
and there's no consideration.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.520
A gift promise is not
an invitation to bargain

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:02.780
because the promiser is offering something

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:05.150
without demanding anything in return.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:07.740
Since no exchange is sought
by the promising party,

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:08.880
there's no consideration.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.910
So for example, a court would not enforce

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:12.290
a bear promise to a friend

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:14.470
that if the promiser ever won the lottery,

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:16.740
then the lottery winnings
would be shared equally

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:17.720
between the friends,

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:21.420
thus a gift promise doesn't
create an enforceable contract.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:23.730
And this was essentially the conclusion

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.130
of a lawsuit against Tonda Dickerson,

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.950
an Alabama Waffle House waitress

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:30.690
who received a Florida
lottery ticket as a tip

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:32.820
that won $10 million.

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:34.950
Two years after winning, Edward Seward,

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.400
the man who left the
lottery ticket as a tip,

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.390
sued Dickerson and her coworkers

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.920
to recover the entire $10 million prize.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:44.250
In his complaint, Seward alleged Dickerson

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:46.930
had promised to buy him a truck
with any lottery winnings,

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:48.040
she didn't do that,

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:51.400
and thus Seward was
claiming to be a victim

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.410
of fraudulent misrepresentation.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:56.030
But instead of seeking to
recover just the truck,

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:57.280
Seward demanded that the court

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.130
invalidate the tip on
the grounds of fraud,

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:02.810
and award him the entire $10
million lottery winnings.

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:04.790
The trial court ruled against Seward

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:07.470
holding that the tip ticket
was an unconditional gift

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:09.270
and found no evidence of fraud.

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:10.450
And in support of their ruling,

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:11.550
the court found the Dickerson

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:13.380
had made no misrepresentations to Seward

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.060
before she received a lottery ticket.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.520
Seward gave Dickerson the ticket

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:18.690
without previously discussing
what would happen if she won,

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.520
not on this occasion, nor
on any previous occasion

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:24.400
of tipping the Waffle House
employees with lottery tickets,

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:27.510
and Dickerson never requested
a lottery ticket from Seward

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:29.210
rather, he just gave it to her freely

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:30.690
and without expectation.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:33.670
And of course, Seward did not
limit his damages to the claim

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.360
that he was entitled to a new truck,

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:38.580
but was claiming the
entire $10 million prize,

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.910
which probably didn't help
his case all that much.

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.390
Seward appealed his case

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:46.120
to the Alabama Supreme Court and lost.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:48.790
So this Reddit case seems
to be pretty similar

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:49.623
to the Seward case,

00:11:49.623 --> 00:11:51.790
both Seward and the Reddit bar patron

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.850
had a pattern of leaving
lottery as tips for servers.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:56.420
There's no indication that either patron

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:57.900
discussed sharing the proceeds

00:11:57.900 --> 00:11:59.560
from winning tickets with their servers,

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:00.620
and there's no indication

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:02.480
that the servers
fraudulently misrepresented

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:04.160
that they would share the winnings

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.310
to induce the patrons to leave
the lottery tickets as a tip.

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:09.530
So based on these facts,

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:11.150
it doesn't seem like the bar patron

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:14.180
has any right to share in
the Redditor's winnings.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.830
Now, maybe the Redditor is holding back.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:18.260
If there was a discussion,

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:19.880
if there was an oral contract,

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.000
that could change the facts of this case.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.190
By the way, this is what
lawyers do all the time.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:26.470
We analogize to old cases,

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:30.140
and we in comparison
to the cases before us.

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:33.210
So whether it's Reddit, or
whether it is an actual lawsuit

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.330
this is the kind of stuff
that we do every day.

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:36.980
And there are certainly facts

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:39.350
that this Redditor might
have left out from the post

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:40.710
that could change this outcome.

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:44.850
But, based on what we know,
I'm sorry to the bar patron

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:46.710
but you are not entitled to anything

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:48.170
from the lottery winnings.

